// Context-switch throughput gate (#1849) — the Bit counterpart of a unit test
// (`context switch: round trip and microbench sanity (<1us/switch)`) that used
// to live in the deleted native runtime's `sched` module.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. Grep the whole of `_tests_/` for a throughput assertion —
// microbench, ns/switch, "per switch" — and before this file there were ZERO
// hits. That one unit test was the repo's ONLY performance-regression gate,
// and it lived in a module deleted along with the rest of that runtime.
// Meanwhile #1849 records a 4-9x slowdown that nothing caught:
//
//     _tests_/stress/quicwire           BIT_GC=stress   38.3s -> 161s
//     _tests_/stress/polloneshotdarwin  BIT_GC=stress   13.1s -> 123s
//
// A suite with no throughput assertion cannot tell "still correct" from "still
// correct, four times slower".
//
// WHAT IT MEASURES, AND HOW IT DIFFERS FROM THE DELETED ORIGINAL. That test
// drove `contextSwitch` directly against a hand-built `Context` on a manually
// mapped stack: a raw register-save/restore microbenchmark with no scheduler in
// the picture. That is unreachable from Bit by construction — `Context`,
// `mapGuardedStack` and `contextSwitch` are runtime internals behind the ABI,
// and a Bit program can only reach the scheduler through `spawn` and channels.
// So this measures one level up: an unbuffered-channel ping-pong between two
// green threads, which is the switch PLUS the park/unpark and run-queue work
// that actually surrounds every switch in a real program. That is the more
// useful quantity anyway — #1849's regressions are in that surrounding work,
// not in the register shuffle — but it is a DIFFERENT number, and a strictly
// larger one. See "coverage" at the bottom.
//
// THE HARD PART IS NOT MEASURING, IT IS NOT BEING FLAKY. A wall-clock
// assertion that reddens on a loaded machine is worse than no assertion at all:
// it teaches people to re-run until green, and then a real regression gets
// re-run too. Two mechanisms make this one hold, both chosen from measurements
// on the development host (Apple M-series, 18 cores) and not from taste:
//
//   1. MINIMUM OF `trials`, NOT MEAN. Contention can only ever make a timing
//      worse, never better, so the minimum of N independent trials is the
//      statistic that noise cannot inflate. Measured at #1849's original gate
//      creation, pre-#2925 codegen: under 72 spinning processes on 18 cores
//      (load average 30+), individual trials degraded to 4478 ns/switch — 10x
//      — while the minimum over 9 trials stayed at 492 against an unloaded
//      421. The mean would have failed; the minimum did not move. #2925
//      (2026-08-13) since dropped the unloaded anchor itself to ~150-157
//      ns/switch (see the ceiling comment below) by removing a per-iteration
//      safepoint call from every compiled loop back edge — a codegen change,
//      not a contention change, so this argument is unaffected: it is about
//      how contention behaves relative to a baseline, not about the baseline's
//      absolute value.
//
//   2. NORMALIZED RATIOS, NOT ABSOLUTE NANOSECONDS. This was the surprise, and
//      it is why an absolute threshold is not merely portable-unfriendly but
//      wrong even on ONE host: over an hour of measurement (#1849, pre-#2925
//      codegen), the same binary's minimum went from 421 to 827 ns/switch — 2x
//      — purely from the machine's background state (the calibration loop
//      slowed by the same 1.9x, so the host really was that much slower, not
//      the scheduler). An absolute bound wide enough to survive that (>=2.5x
//      headroom) can no longer see a 4x regression. #2971 re-baselined the
//      ratio ceilings below after #2925 moved the absolute anchor (see the
//      false-red note at the bottom of this file); the ratio design itself
//      needed no change, which is exactly the point this experiment made. So
//      the assertion is on ratios against work measured in the SAME process,
//      in the SAME trial loop:
//
//        cpuRatio   round-trip cost / cost of a fixed integer loop.
//                   Cancels host CPU speed, thermal state and contention.
//        chanRatio  round-trip cost / cost of the same number of buffered
//                   channel send+recv pairs on ONE thread, which exercise the
//                   same `chanSend`/`chanRecv` code with no switch in it.
//                   Cancels everything cpuRatio does AND any change in channel
//                   bookkeeping cost, isolating the park/switch/unpark path.
//
//      Both ratios held within ~10% across every condition measured, including
//      the 2x host slowdown and the 4x-oversubscribed load, where the absolute
//      number moved by 2x and 10x respectively.
//
// Two assertions rather than one because each covers the other's blind spot: a
// regression that slows ALL channel traffic (buffered included) leaves
// chanRatio flat and is caught by cpuRatio; a compiler codegen change that
// alters the integer-loop baseline moves cpuRatio and is caught — as a
// contradiction between the two — by chanRatio staying put. Both numbers are
// printed unconditionally on success as well as failure, so a run's output is a
// usable data point rather than just a verdict.
//
// WHAT IT WILL AND WILL NOT CATCH, measured rather than asserted. Inserting
// relay green threads into the ping-pong chain makes each MEASURED switch cost
// a known multiple of real scheduler work — no injected sleeps, no synthetic
// delay, just more park/unpark per round trip — which is a faithful stand-in
// for "the scheduler got N times slower". Re-measured for #2971
// (2026-08-13, post-#2925 codegen, arm64, idle box: `pgrep -fl make-driver`
// empty, load average 2.6-3.4 and falling):
//
//     relays   true slowdown   cpuRatioX10    chanRatioX10   verdict
//        1          1.5x          2220             71          pass
//        2          2.0x        2952-3012         96-97         pass
//        3          2.5x        3629-3647        118-121        FAIL
//        6          4.0x          6062            197           FAIL
//     (clean baseline, min of 3: cpuRatioX10 1396-1478, chanRatioX10 46-48)
//
// Those cpuRatioX10 figures and their verdicts are #2971's, against the 3100
// ceiling and 1396 baseline of that day; what carries forward is the mapping,
// not the absolutes. Relay count n multiplies the switches per round trip by
// (2 + n) / 2, and the ceiling is always ~2.2x the host's own measured
// baseline, so the detection floor sits at ~2.1x on EVERY host target by
// construction — #2925 raising the baseline raised the ceiling by the same
// factor and left the floor where it was (previously ~2.3x), and #5632
// splitting the ceiling per target does the same thing three times.
//
// #5632 re-ran the sweep's two informative points against the per-target
// ceilings below, at main ce844a9d, one toolchain (the Linux binaries
// cross-built with `bit build -t`), 3 clean runs per host first:
//
//     host target     clean cpuRatioX10   1 relay (1.5x)   3 relays (2.5x)
//     aarch64-macos      873-891 pass      1382 pass        2148 FAIL
//     aarch64-linux    2290-2311 pass         —             5805 FAIL
//     x86_64-linux     5265-5629 pass         —            12952 FAIL
//
// So the #1849 class (4-9x) fails by a wide margin on all three, 2.5x fails on
// all three, and 1.5x still passes — the same deliberate price #1849 paid.
// Neither sweep repeats #1849's host-load and 72-spinning-process conditions,
// which need a loaded box and are orthogonal to codegen drift — see the
// MINIMUM-OF-`trials` note above for why that mechanism does not depend on the
// absolute anchor moving.
//
// FALSE RED, HOW TO READ ONE. `cpuRatio` high and `chanRatio` normal, with
// `spin` far faster than the recorded baseline, is a codegen improvement to the
// calibration loop, not a scheduler regression — re-baseline this host
// target's `maxCpuRatioX10*` anchor. `cpuRatio` high on ONE host target while
// `chanRatio` sits where the other targets read it is the shape #5632 found:
// the ratio's denominator is a register-resident loop and its numerator is not,
// so a host whose non-register work costs more relative to its integer
// throughput reads high with nothing wrong — re-derive THAT target's anchor
// from both terms, and never widen the others to cover it.
// `chanRatio` high and `cpuRatio` normal, with `buffered` far faster than the
// baseline recorded beside `maxChanRatioX10` and `ns/switch` at or below its
// own baseline, is the same shape one level over: the buffered channel path
// got faster, not the scheduler slower — re-derive `maxChanRatioX10` from BOTH
// terms at one commit (#4750), never raise it alone. Both ratios high is a
// real scheduler regression. Both normal but the absolute `ns/switch` far
// above the baseline is a slow or busy host, and is exactly the case these
// ratios exist to not fail on.

import { monotonic, since, sleep } from "std/time"
import { exit } from "std/os"

// --- tunables ---------------------------------------------------------------

// Round trips per timed run. 20k is 17-33ms here depending on host state: long
// enough that timer granularity and one-off scheduling hiccups are noise, short
// enough that all 15 trials of all three measurements cost ~1.2s.
const rounds: int = 20000

// Iterations of the integer calibration loop, sized to cost the same order as
// one ping-pong run so neither dominates the trial.
const spins: int = 2000000

// Independent trials; the minimum of each series is the reported figure. 15
// costs ~1.2s and gives the minimum enough chances that sustained contention
// has to hit EVERY trial to move it.
const trials: int = 15

// Ceilings, as fixed point scaled by 10. Justified above and in the numbers
// below; both were measured with this exact harness — cpuRatioX10 on the
// development host, chanRatioX10 on the host that reads highest.
//
//   cpuRatioX10   integer-loop iterations of work per context switch.
//                 ONE CEILING PER HOST TARGET (#5632). This ratio does not
//                 transfer across architectures, and asserting a single
//                 number everywhere is what made this gate red on
//                 x86_64-linux with nothing wrong. All three anchors were
//                 re-derived at ONE commit (main ce844a9d, 2026-09-19) with
//                 ONE toolchain — the two Linux binaries cross-built on the
//                 macOS box with `bit build -t <triple>`, so the compiler
//                 that lowered the denominator is identical in all three.
//                 Every run below is itself a min of 15 trials; the anchor is
//                 the min of the runs, and the ceiling is this file's
//                 standing formula, ceil(2.2 * min / 100) * 100:
//
//                   host target     runs (cpuRatioX10)         min  ceiling
//                   aarch64-macos   837 853 862 845            837     1900
//                   aarch64-linux   2312 2308 2315 2291       2291     5100
//                   x86_64-linux    5254 5076 5073 5456 5499  5073    11200
//
//                 WHY THEY DIVERGE 6x — measured, not assumed. Everything in
//                 the runtime's own cost class scales together and the
//                 calibration loop does not. x86_64-linux against
//                 aarch64-linux at that commit: ping-pong 1193 vs 213
//                 ns/switch (5.6x), buffered channel op 22.3 vs 4.2 ns
//                 (5.3x), process user time 0.56 vs 0.10s (5.6x) — but the
//                 integer loop only 2.17 vs 0.94 ns/iter (2.3x). That is why
//                 `chanRatioX10`, whose numerator and denominator are both
//                 runtime channel code, is FLAT across the two (253-254 vs
//                 259-274) while `cpuRatioX10`, which divides runtime cost by
//                 a register-only loop, carries a 2.4x offset with no
//                 regression in it.
//                 It is NOT the park/wake path: `BIT_WORKERS=1` reads the
//                 same as the default (1232 vs 1238 ns/switch), and the whole
//                 0.85s x86_64 run makes 413 voluntary context switches for
//                 600000 green ones — there is no syscall in the loop to be
//                 taxed. It is NOT that box being bad at scheduling either: a
//                 non-Bit python3 probe in the same two gate images has the
//                 x86_64 box FASTER at a kernel pipe round trip (4794 vs 5889
//                 ns/switch) and at a cross-core cache-line handoff (45.9 vs
//                 60.0 ns). What is left is codegen for everything that is
//                 not a register-resident loop, the same 3.5-5x x86_64 band
//                 #2935 already records.
//                 THE HEADROOM IS LOAD, NOT SLACK: on the macOS box under 10
//                 spinning processes (18 cores) the anchor moved 837 -> 883,
//                 915, +5-9% against 2.2x of room.
//                 History: the single 3100 was #2971's (2026-08-13, arm64
//                 macOS, idle: min of 3 runs 1434, 1478, 1396 -> 1396), and
//                 by #5632 it had drifted to 3.7x that host's real figure.
//                 The pre-#2925 316-355 band is NOT comparable — #2925
//                 inlined the back-edge safepoint guard, which sped up the
//                 compiled calibration loop (this ratio's own denominator)
//                 ~11x with the scheduler unchanged (#2971).
//   chanRatioX10  buffered send+recv pairs per context switch.
//                 Both terms are pinned-stage0 runtime code, so unlike
//                 cpuRatioX10 this ratio is not sensitive to compiler-under-
//                 test codegen — but it IS sensitive to the runtime's own
//                 buffered chanSend/chanRecv cost, which is its DENOMINATOR.
//                 #4750 re-baseline (2026-09-11, both terms measured at main
//                 84d21fdf): aarch64-linux under scripts/arm64gate.sh, box
//                 drained, boxlock solo, three runs -> chanRatioX10 256, 252,
//                 266 -> min 252, from a ping-pong of 208-209 ns/switch over
//                 a 3.91-4.14 ns buffered channel op (buffered 163417ns,
//                 165500ns, 156500ns for 40000 ops). Ceiling =
//                 ceil(2.2 * 252 / 10) * 10 = 560 — the cpuRatioX10 formula
//                 above at this ratio's scale, quantum 10 rather than 100.
//                 Derived on aarch64-linux because it reads highest: the same
//                 commit gives 90 on macOS/arm64 (buffered 171167ns) and 78
//                 on x86_64-linux (buffered 713586ns), so 560 holds on all
//                 three with the arm64-linux term as the binding one.
//                 The old 240 was #1591's ~2.1x over an "observed 93-113"
//                 band and went stale from the denominator, not the
//                 scheduler: buffered was 606791ns at 8db6e7be (2026-08-13)
//                 and is ~160000ns here, a 3.7x speedup of the buffered path,
//                 so the same switch cost divides by a 3.7x smaller number
//                 (#4736, #4750). Raising this number without BOTH terms
//                 measured at one commit and recorded here is what let it go
//                 stale; do not.
//
// Both ceilings are ~2.2x the measured baseline by construction (see the
// formulas above); the sweep above shows a 2.5x scheduler slowdown still
// crosses cpuRatioX10 with headroom.
const maxCpuRatioX10Arm64Macos: int = 1900
const maxCpuRatioX10Arm64Linux: int = 5100
const maxCpuRatioX10X64Linux: int = 11200
const maxChanRatioX10: int = 560

// `hostTarget()`'s ordinals are 0 = x86_64-linux, 1 = aarch64-linux,
// 2 = aarch64-macos (SPEC §11.8). A target with no anchor of its own gets the
// TIGHTEST of the three rather than the loosest: a new host reading red is a
// question someone answers with a measurement, a new host reading green
// because nothing was ever derived for it is a gate that is not there.
fn maxCpuRatioX10For(target: int): int {
  if (target == 0) {
    return maxCpuRatioX10X64Linux
  }
  if (target == 1) {
    return maxCpuRatioX10Arm64Linux
  }
  return maxCpuRatioX10Arm64Macos
}

// Converts a deadlock into a failure instead of an infinite stall (docs/development.md:
// "a hang is a failure, not a stall"). This harness spawns no child process, so
// the per-child deadline does not cover it; the guard has to be in-process.
// 120s is ~100x the measured runtime — far above anything a merely slow host
// produces, so firing it means "stuck", never "busy".
const watchdogNs: int = 120000000000

// --- failure ----------------------------------------------------------------

fn benchFail(msg: string) {
  eprint("FAIL schedbench: " + msg + "\n")
  exit(1)
}

// Parks on the scheduler's timer queue, so it costs no worker while it waits
// and cannot itself perturb the measurement. If `main` returns first the
// process exits and this never runs.
fn benchWatchdog() {
  sleep(watchdogNs)
  eprint("FAIL schedbench: no result after 120s — the ping-pong deadlocked\n")
  exit(1)
}

// --- the measurement --------------------------------------------------------

// Echoes exactly `n` values back, then reports how many it saw. Each `<- a`
// and each `b <- v` on an unbuffered channel is a park + switch, so one loop
// iteration here is one round trip and two context switches.
fn benchPong(a: chan<int>, b: chan<int>, done: chan<int>, n: int) {
  let seen: int = 0
  while (seen < n) {
    let v = <- a
    b <- v
    seen = seen + 1
  }
  done <- seen
}

// One timed ping-pong run: `n` round trips over unbuffered channels, returning
// elapsed nanoseconds. Every echoed value is checked against what was sent and
// the partner's own count is checked at the end — a channel that dropped,
// duplicated or short-circuited traffic would otherwise post a spectacular
// throughput number, which is the classic way a benchmark-shaped gate becomes
// vacuous.
fn benchRoundTrips(n: int): int {
  let a = chan<int>(0)
  let b = chan<int>(0)
  let done = chan<int>(0)
  spawn benchPong(a, b, done, n)

  let start = monotonic()
  let i: int = 0
  while (i < n) {
    a <- i
    let v = <- b
    if (v != i) {
      benchFail("ping-pong echoed ${v} where ${i} was sent")
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  let elapsed = since(start)

  let seen = <- done
  if (seen != n) {
    benchFail("partner thread handled ${seen} round trips, expected ${n}")
  }
  return elapsed
}

// Pure integer work, no channel and no scheduler: the host-speed baseline.
// Returns elapsed nanoseconds; the accumulator is returned through a checked
// comparison so the loop cannot be optimized away.
fn benchSpinWork(n: int): int {
  let acc: int = 0
  let i: int = 0
  while (i < n) {
    acc = acc + (i ^ (acc >> 3)) + 1
    i = i + 1
  }
  return acc
}

fn benchSpin(n: int): int {
  let start = monotonic()
  let acc = benchSpinWork(n)
  let elapsed = since(start)
  if (acc == 0) {
    benchFail("calibration loop produced 0 — it was elided")
  }
  return elapsed
}

// `n` send+recv pairs on a capacity-1 channel from a single thread. The buffer
// is empty at every send and full at every receive, so neither ever blocks:
// this is `chanSend`/`chanRecv` with the park/switch/unpark path removed, which
// is precisely the difference the ping-pong measures.
fn benchBuffered(n: int): int {
  let c = chan<int>(1)
  let start = monotonic()
  let i: int = 0
  while (i < n) {
    c <- i
    let v = <- c
    if (v != i) {
      benchFail("buffered channel returned ${v} where ${i} was sent")
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return since(start)
}

// --- driver -----------------------------------------------------------------

// Runs the three measurements `trials` times each, interleaved so that any
// slow period on the host lands on all three rather than on one, and returns
// the minimum of each series: (ping-pong, spin, buffered) nanoseconds.
fn benchMin(a: int, b: int): int {
  if (a < b) {
    return a
  }
  return b
}

fn benchTrials(): (int, int, int) {
  // Seeded above any measurement the watchdog would ever let through (1000s
  // against a 120s deadline), so the first trial always wins and no
  // first-iteration special case is needed. The guard after the loop proves a
  // real measurement replaced it rather than the seed leaking into the result.
  let bestSwitch: int = 1000000000000
  let bestSpin: int = 1000000000000
  let bestBuf: int = 1000000000000

  let t: int = 0
  while (t < trials) {
    let sw = benchRoundTrips(rounds)
    let sp = benchSpin(spins)
    let bf = benchBuffered(rounds)
    if (sw <= 0 || sp <= 0 || bf <= 0) {
      benchFail("monotonic clock did not advance (switch=${sw} spin=${sp} buf=${bf})")
    }
    bestSwitch = benchMin(bestSwitch, sw)
    bestSpin = benchMin(bestSpin, sp)
    bestBuf = benchMin(bestBuf, bf)
    t = t + 1
  }
  if (bestSwitch >= 1000000000000 || bestSpin >= 1000000000000 || bestBuf >= 1000000000000) {
    benchFail("no trial recorded a measurement (trials=${trials})")
  }
  return bestSwitch, bestSpin, bestBuf
}

fn main() {
  spawn benchWatchdog()
  let (bestSwitch, bestSpin, bestBuf) = benchTrials()

  // Two switches per round trip, matching the deleted original's convention.
  let switches = rounds * 2
  let nsPerSwitch = bestSwitch / switches
  let cpuRatioX10 = bestSwitch * spins * 10 / (bestSpin * switches)
  let chanRatioX10 = bestSwitch * 10 / (bestBuf * 2)

  let maxCpu = maxCpuRatioX10For(hostTarget())
  print("schedbench: ${nsPerSwitch} ns/switch, cpuRatio ${cpuRatioX10}/10 (max ${maxCpu}/10), chanRatio ${chanRatioX10}/10 (max ${maxChanRatioX10}/10)\n")
  print("schedbench: best of ${trials} trials — pingpong ${bestSwitch}ns, spin ${bestSpin}ns, buffered ${bestBuf}ns\n")

  if (cpuRatioX10 > maxCpu) {
    benchFail("context switch costs ${cpuRatioX10}/10 calibration iterations, ceiling is ${maxCpu}/10 for this host target (${nsPerSwitch} ns/switch)")
  }
  if (chanRatioX10 > maxChanRatioX10) {
    benchFail("context switch costs ${chanRatioX10}/10 buffered channel ops, ceiling is ${maxChanRatioX10}/10 (${nsPerSwitch} ns/switch)")
  }
  print("OK schedbench\n")
}

// COVERAGE RELATIVE TO THE DELETED ORIGINAL. The original also asserted
// `iterations == round_trips`, that the partner fiber really ran; the
// equivalent here is the `done` count plus the per-value echo check, and it is
// stricter, since the original's counter could not detect reordering. What is
// NOT reproduced is the bare `contextSwitch` path: the original built a
// `Context` by hand and switched into a manually mapped guarded stack with no
// task, no run queue and no channel, so it measured register save/restore
// alone and would catch a regression confined to the assembly. Here that cost
// is a fraction of a round trip that also includes park, unpark, queueing and
// possibly a cross-worker wakeup, so an assembly-only regression is diluted.
// Reaching it needs runtime internals a Bit program cannot address; the
// bare-switch number, if it is ever wanted, has to come from a Bit runtime
// benchmark on the runtime side of the ABI, not from here.
